🌐 The Mission: You type www.instagram.com and hit Enter

Here's the thing — computers don't understand "instagram.com," they only understand IP addresses (like 157.240.12.35). So DNS's whole JOB is to convert that human-friendly name into a machine-friendly number. 📞➡️🔢

🕵️ Step-by-Step: The Full Chase
Step 1: Check your own Browser Cache 🧠

"Wait, have I visited this site recently? Let me check my memory first."

Browser checks if it already resolved this domain recently (cached). If yes → done, skip everything below! ⚡

Step 2: Check OS Cache 💻

"Browser doesn't remember? Let me (the computer) check MY memory."

If not in browser cache, the Operating System checks its own DNS cache.

Step 3: Ask the Resolver (usually your ISP) 📞

"Neither of us remembers? Fine, let's call in a professional."

If still no luck, the request goes to a DNS Resolver (usually run by your ISP, or public ones like Google's 8.8.8.8). This resolver is like your personal assistant who does all the legwork below on your behalf.

Now the Resolver goes on ITS OWN detective mission 🔎
Step 4: Ask a Root Server 🌍

Resolver: "Yo, I need instagram.com's IP. Where do I even start?"
Root Server: "I don't know the exact address, but go ask the '.com' guy, he handles all .com domains."

There are 13 root server clusters worldwide — they don't know exact answers, they just point you in the right direction. Like asking a mall directory "which floor is Zara on?"

Step 5: Ask the TLD Server (.com, .org, .in etc) 🏢

Resolver: "Hey .com server, where's instagram.com?"
TLD Server: "I don't have the exact IP either, but go ask Instagram's own official Name Server — here's their address."

Step 6: Ask the Authoritative Name Server 🎯

Resolver: "Hey, YOU'RE Instagram's official record-keeper right? What's your IP?"
Name Server: "Yes! Here you go: 157.240.12.35" ✅

THIS is the guy who actually KNOWS the real answer — because Instagram's own DNS records live here.

Step 7: Resolver sends IP back to your browser 📬

Resolver: "Got it! Here's the IP: 157.240.12.35"

Step 8: Browser connects using that IP 🚀

Now your browser does a TCP handshake (remember that from before? 😉) with 157.240.12.35, and finally... Instagram loads! 🎉